            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Economic Policies</strong><br>\nLouis XIV's economic policies were influenced by his minister of finance, Jean-Baptiste Colbert. Colbert implemented mercantilist policies to boost France's economy, strengthening industries like tapestry manufacturing, and increasing colonial trade. However, despite these efforts, the monarchy's extravagant spending led to high debts, which were exacerbated by Louis's costly wars.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Campaigns</strong><br>\nLouis XIV's reign is marked by numerous military campaigns aimed at expanding French territory and influence. He successfully acquired territories such as Franche-Comté and parts of the Spanish Netherlands. These victories, however, came at a great cost, leading to continuous conflicts like the War of the Spanish Succession, which drained French resources and manpower.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Palace of Versailles</strong><br>\nOne of Louis XIV's most famous legacies is the Palace of Versailles, a symbol of royal absolutism and classical French art. He transformed a hunting lodge into this magnificent palace, a project that consumed much of France's resources. The palace was not only his residence but also the seat of France's government by the end of his reign, where he hosted lavish parties and conducted state affairs.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
