          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Religious Policy</strong><br>\nLouis XIV upheld the Catholic faith as the sole religion of France, revoking the Edict of Nantes with the Edict of Fontainebleau in 1685. This action led to the persecution of Huguenots and the emigration of many Protestants, which deprived France of many skilled and industrious workers. The king's religious policies were part of his larger aim to unify France under one king, one law, and one faith.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Other European Powers</strong><br>\nThroughout his reign, Louis XIV maintained complex relationships with other European powers, including alliances and rivalries. His aggressive foreign policies and expansionist ambitions led to a coalition of European powers against France at various times. His wars and the resulting treaties shaped the political landscape of Europe and established France as a preeminent European power.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nLouis XIV died on September 1, 1715, just days before his 77th birthday. His long reign had seen France emerge as a leading European power in arts, culture, and politics. However, his death also left France in debt and surrounded by enemies, setting the stage for future struggles. Louis XIV's legacy includes his contribution to consolidating the monarchy's power, which influenced European history for generations.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
